From: François Guerraz <kubrick@fgv6.net>
Subject: [PATCH] net: ethernet: qualcomm: ppe: S-R mode engine

The S-R mode is the one chip-wide alternative to profile E while the
switch runs the rtl8_4s tagger: while it is not off it owns the egress
S-TPID, the CPU port's egress tag modes and the conduit's parser role and
L3 binding, and E takes no flow. This patch adds the mode around the
register sequences of the previous one: arming, offers on and off,
disarming, FAULT and every automatic teardown, and the interface the
controls of a later patch drive. S-R offers themselves are still
declined: with this patch the mode can be armed and disarmed, and E,
WAN and every other flow is judged against it.

ppe_sr_eng.c holds the mode in pure code (tested in user space, like the
sequences):

- a generation, bumped under the offload lock by every change an offer
  depends on (each state change, a topology or scope change, a resync, a
  publish), read lockless with acquire. Every offer takes a ticket first
  thing (the generation, then the mode: E while S-R is off, S-R while it
  is active, none in between, declined) and may write hardware only if,
  under the lock, the generation is unchanged and the state still gives
  its mode. An E download judged while S-R was off is therefore never
  added once the arm has started, nor after it rolled back;
- the arm's checks (off, not refused at boot, a deadline, at least one
  class in scope, at most 48, no truncated L3 device list), the
  sequences' arm, and offers on if asked; offers on and off; the disarm,
  counted by trigger; each with its log line;
- the degraded hook: every place the offload latches degraded or loses
  a flow entry now goes through ppe_offload_set_degraded(), which moves a
  mode that is not off to FAULT (claim, registers and identities kept
  until reboot). A latch during a sequence's flush is reported to it as
  a flow left behind, so that the sequence stops there as for a stuck
  flow (released and off during an arm, FAULT otherwise);
- topology events: dirty marks, a generation bump, the ports' flows
  dropped; the resync (the identity diff and S5) clears them, or
  disarms on a failure; nothing moves in FAULT;
- the statistics-time switch check: while active, a switch that left
  READY queues one disarm;
- the boot check at enable (S9) on every EDMA port: S-R registers not
  at their base refuse S-R until reboot;
- the scope (the classes that get an identity, the client addresses),
  the plan from it, with qcom_ppe.sr_u_uploads=0 giving U classes no
  identity, and the deadline arithmetic: an arm's deadline is capped at
  the switch's own less 30 s and must leave 60 s; a later one only
  moves forward.

ppe_sr.c binds it to the kernel: the io over the PPE regmap, the
offload's flush, and the conduit's rtl8_4s state word, looked up per use
under RCU on the held conduit (NULL: the switch is gone, skipped and
said); ppe_sr_arm() (RTNL, then the lock, held from the prechecks to
the end, so the topology resolved cannot change before the identities
are written; the conduit is held from the claim until the mode is off
again); ppe_sr_offers(), ppe_sr_disarm() and ppe_sr_disarm_locked_by();
the conduit going down or away, an S-R user port or the S-R bridge going
away disarm, and in FAULT the conduit's reference goes at its
unregister; changes of the ports, the bridge, its L3 devices and the
conduit's MTU or address are topology events, resynced from the ordered
FDB workqueue once complete; a reboot notifier disarms before the
switch's shutdown, waiting at most 2 s for the lock; the scope and
deadline setters, a memory-only status and an idle register verify with
the identities' counters.

In ppe_offload.c: disabling offload (parameter, trip, unload) disarms
first, and keeps everything if the mode is in FAULT; the conduit's L3
binding, rewritten by an MTU or address change, is left alone while S-R
holds the port (edma_port_l3_if_program() goes through
ppe_offload_port_l3_if_bind()); offload_flows shows the S-R state.

Signed-off-by: François Guerraz <kubrick@fgv6.net>
